00:00:53.101 --> 00:00:55.600
and has figured out how to hook
it up to several other cloud

00:00:55.600 --> 00:00:59.980
technologies to extract the
maximum value from Stackdriver

00:00:59.980 --> 00:01:01.450
Logging.

00:01:01.450 --> 00:01:03.380
So I'll just give
a quick overview

00:01:03.380 --> 00:01:05.202
of what we're
going to talk about

00:01:05.202 --> 00:01:06.910
and what the technology
is, and then I'll

00:01:06.910 --> 00:01:09.226
hand it over to Romain.

00:01:09.226 --> 00:01:10.600
So first we're
just going to talk

00:01:10.600 --> 00:01:12.760
about Stackdriver
Logging in Apps Script,

00:01:12.760 --> 00:01:14.590
and how the integration works.

00:01:14.590 --> 00:01:17.620
Romain will give an example
based on Yet Another Mail

00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:21.345
Merge, which is a product
that he has built and manages.

00:01:21.345 --> 00:01:22.720
And we're going
to talk about how

00:01:22.720 --> 00:01:26.020
you get your logs from
Stackdriver into BigQuery

00:01:26.020 --> 00:01:31.470
for analysis, and do a lot
more powerful stuff with them.

00:01:31.470 --> 00:01:33.190
And then another example--

00:01:33.190 --> 00:01:36.400
a Firebase example-- and
then finally Data Studio.

00:01:36.400 --> 00:01:39.130
So Romain has really
done our work for us

00:01:39.130 --> 00:01:41.050
in identifying all
of the ways that you

00:01:41.050 --> 00:01:43.952
can leverage this data once
you get it in to Stackdriver.

00:01:46.790 --> 00:01:52.940
So the basics are that we now
define console dot star methods

00:01:52.940 --> 00:01:53.790
--

00:01:53.790 --> 00:01:56.750
the familiar logging methods
from the JavaScript client

00:01:56.750 --> 00:01:58.520
in the browser--

00:01:58.520 --> 00:02:02.660
and messages-- and I should
also say objects and values

00:02:02.660 --> 00:02:06.470
of other types that
you log to console, get

00:02:06.470 --> 00:02:10.360
sent to Stackdriver Logging.

00:02:10.360 --> 00:02:14.380
You can also configure Apps
Script to log exceptions

00:02:14.380 --> 00:02:15.940
to Stackdriver Logging.

00:02:15.940 --> 00:02:17.950
This is particularly
powerful if you're

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:20.837
building an add-on or something
that has a large user base.

00:02:20.837 --> 00:02:22.420
You want to keep
track of whether it's

00:02:22.420 --> 00:02:24.044
working for people
other than yourself,

00:02:24.044 --> 00:02:26.026
in environments
other than your own.

00:02:26.026 --> 00:02:27.400
And the existing
logging solution

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:29.570
is frankly not up to snuff.

00:02:29.570 --> 00:02:31.977
It captures the most
recent set of logs.

00:02:31.977 --> 00:02:34.060
And so when you're working
with a large user base,

00:02:34.060 --> 00:02:36.220
you really need
something richer.

00:02:36.220 --> 00:02:38.020
And so here in
project properties

00:02:38.020 --> 00:02:41.040
you can check the
exceptions box,

00:02:41.040 --> 00:02:42.910
and you're off and running.

00:02:46.010 --> 00:02:49.700
Here in the view menu
there's a new menu item

00:02:49.700 --> 00:02:51.950
for people participating
in the EAP,

00:02:51.950 --> 00:02:56.820
or when the feature rolls out,
to view your console logs.

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:03.710
And here you see logs in the
Stackdriver Logging interface--

00:03:03.710 --> 00:03:05.450
a couple of hints
at other cool stuff

00:03:05.450 --> 00:03:08.380
you can do, creating
metrics and exports.

00:03:08.380 --> 00:03:12.200
And then you can also filter
by either the metadata

00:03:12.200 --> 00:03:15.420
that we send along with the
message, or the message itself

00:03:15.420 --> 00:03:18.245
if you're sending adjacent
object as your log message.

00:03:22.370 --> 00:03:24.010
We also, automatically,
in addition

00:03:24.010 --> 00:03:25.970
to some other metadata,
we automatically

00:03:25.970 --> 00:03:30.260
tag every log entry
with a user key.

00:03:30.260 --> 00:03:33.190
And this is done
for two purposes.

00:03:33.190 --> 00:03:36.490
Firstly, it just makes it
easy for you to see what's

00:03:36.490 --> 00:03:38.860
happening to a particular user.

00:03:38.860 --> 00:03:41.590
The other reason that
we do this is as kind

00:03:41.590 --> 00:03:43.540
of a serving suggestion.

00:03:43.540 --> 00:03:47.080
These keys are--
don't contain PII,

00:03:47.080 --> 00:03:48.850
they're based on
the user's identity,

00:03:48.850 --> 00:03:50.320
and they rotate once a month.

00:03:50.320 --> 00:03:55.300
And so this is kind of extending
a Google practice to you

00:03:55.300 --> 00:03:59.380
to use if you like, to track
users for short periods of time

00:03:59.380 --> 00:04:01.720
and make sure the application
is working for them,

00:04:01.720 --> 00:04:04.450
but to protect their privacy by
not tracking them over a really

00:04:04.450 --> 00:04:06.580
long period of time.

00:04:06.580 --> 00:04:09.070
You can log other keys--
you can log the user's email

00:04:09.070 --> 00:04:10.730
address, for example.

00:04:10.730 --> 00:04:12.650
Your script has access to it.

00:04:12.650 --> 00:04:14.680
And so this is
something that you

00:04:14.680 --> 00:04:17.980
would use for support
and triaging issues,

00:04:17.980 --> 00:04:20.222
and it's a great way to
do that in a way that

00:04:20.222 --> 00:04:21.430
protects your users' privacy.

00:04:24.700 --> 00:04:28.810
And I'll hand it off to Romain
for the rest of the show.

00:04:28.810 --> 00:04:31.300
ROMAIN VIALARD: Thank you, Paul.

00:04:31.300 --> 00:04:35.860
OK, so-- I'm going to
present how I will--

00:04:35.860 --> 00:04:38.670
how I have started
to use Stackdriver

00:04:38.670 --> 00:04:43.480
Logging with my add-on,
Yet Another Mail Merge.

00:04:43.480 --> 00:04:47.810
So Yet Another Mail
Merge is an add-on

00:04:47.810 --> 00:04:51.130
for Google sheets, a
self sufficient add-on,

00:04:51.130 --> 00:04:57.820
meaning that it doesn't
connect to any other service.

00:04:57.820 --> 00:05:02.320
It really lives
only as an add-on.

00:05:02.320 --> 00:05:04.990
It's a mail merge
add-on, so it lets

00:05:04.990 --> 00:05:11.080
you send email campaigns using
Gmail and Google Spreadsheets.

00:05:11.080 --> 00:05:17.620
It's one of the top add-ons
for Google Spreadsheets.

00:05:17.620 --> 00:05:27.190
So this means that we are
now at 700,000 installations.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:33.460
So 700,000 people have Yet
Another Mail Merge installed

00:05:33.460 --> 00:05:37.340
on their Gmail Google account.

00:05:37.340 --> 00:05:43.660
But we do have a great number
of monthly active users, more

00:05:43.660 --> 00:05:49.590
than 80,000 monthly users,
sending email campaign

00:05:49.590 --> 00:05:52.450
with Yet Another Mail
Merge every month,

00:05:52.450 --> 00:05:59.000
for a total of more than 40
million emails sent monthly.

00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:03.790
And all of that
means that we do have

00:06:03.790 --> 00:06:09.040
to provide support to a lot
of people using this tool,

00:06:09.040 --> 00:06:12.100
even if it's quite
simple to use.

00:06:12.100 --> 00:06:18.460
Many people still need help
or still have issues with it.

00:06:18.460 --> 00:06:22.510
This logging tool, like
Stackdriver Logging,

00:06:22.510 --> 00:06:26.200
is really helpful
to investigate--

00:06:26.200 --> 00:06:28.080
better understand--

00:06:28.080 --> 00:06:33.830
all of the steps a user went
through before he had an issue.

00:06:33.830 --> 00:06:37.100
So I will demonstrate
how we're using

00:06:37.100 --> 00:06:43.270
that now to help in our
support and in other part

00:06:43.270 --> 00:06:46.300
of the development
of the add-on.

00:06:46.300 --> 00:06:51.136
So first, just to present
a bit, Yet Another Mail

00:06:51.136 --> 00:06:54.230
Merge-- so it's
quite simple to use.

00:06:54.230 --> 00:06:57.610
You simply start--
so it's a tool

00:06:57.610 --> 00:07:01.480
to create a mail merge
campaign, email campaign, so

00:07:01.480 --> 00:07:06.370
to send the same email to a
bunch of different people.

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:10.840
You start by creating
a draft in Gmail,

00:07:10.840 --> 00:07:14.530
where you will be able to
place some place holders that

00:07:14.530 --> 00:07:17.830
will be replaced by data
from your spreadsheet.

00:07:17.830 --> 00:07:20.890
So, for example, here
we have a place holder

00:07:20.890 --> 00:07:24.800
for first name here
and in the spreadsheet,

00:07:24.800 --> 00:07:28.350
you can see that in the
first column, column A,

00:07:28.350 --> 00:07:31.720
there's a column
named First Name.

00:07:31.720 --> 00:07:37.292
So for each row of
the spreadsheet, Yet

00:07:37.292 --> 00:07:40.850
Another Mail Merge
will send an email

00:07:40.850 --> 00:07:45.040
and will reuse our
template, the email draft,

00:07:45.040 --> 00:07:48.340
and replace in
these draft markers

00:07:48.340 --> 00:07:51.190
by the data from
the spreadsheet.

00:07:51.190 --> 00:07:54.940
So to install Yet Another
Mail Merge in a spreadsheet,

00:07:54.940 --> 00:07:57.610
you just have to go
to the add-on store.

00:07:57.610 --> 00:08:00.550
So if you had the menu
add-ons, get add-ons,

00:08:00.550 --> 00:08:04.460
and you will see Yet Another
Mail Merge pretty easily.

00:08:04.460 --> 00:08:07.250
It's, as I said, one
of the top add-ons,

00:08:07.250 --> 00:08:11.860
usually it's even with
the top six add-ons

00:08:11.860 --> 00:08:17.630
that you can find directly
when launching the gallery.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:23.570
After that, so, add-ons are
[INAUDIBLE] a specific menu

00:08:23.570 --> 00:08:27.440
in each spreadsheets, and
for Yet Another Mail Merge,

00:08:27.440 --> 00:08:30.760
you have several menus to--

00:08:30.760 --> 00:08:33.230
to trigger the add-on functions.

00:08:33.230 --> 00:08:37.760
So you can either directly start
the merge if you already have

00:08:37.760 --> 00:08:42.250
a spreadsheet containing
contacts-- recipients--

00:08:42.250 --> 00:08:47.690
or, you can use one of our
integration to import contacts,

00:08:47.690 --> 00:08:52.020
either from Google Contacts or
from cell phones, and so on.

00:08:52.020 --> 00:08:54.680
So check with the--
configure from submission --

00:08:54.680 --> 00:08:59.430
while different--
other features.

00:08:59.430 --> 00:09:04.970
So you-- you indicate
everything you want--

00:09:04.970 --> 00:09:09.860
mostly you select the
draft you want to reuse

00:09:09.860 --> 00:09:11.520
to send your email campaigns.

00:09:11.520 --> 00:09:15.570
So we list all drafts
from your Gmail account.

00:09:15.570 --> 00:09:20.930
And then you can,
for example, set

00:09:20.930 --> 00:09:24.320
if you want to track emails
opened, clicked, [? burned, ?]

00:09:24.320 --> 00:09:25.430
and so on.

00:09:25.430 --> 00:09:28.060
If you want to set
up a specific areas,

00:09:28.060 --> 00:09:29.810
add some personalized
attachments,

00:09:29.810 --> 00:09:34.490
and many other options that
we added over the years

00:09:34.490 --> 00:09:36.290
for the add-on.

00:09:36.290 --> 00:09:40.190
And once you've sent your
campaign into spreadsheets

00:09:40.190 --> 00:09:44.570
directly, you are able to see
the results of your campaign

00:09:44.570 --> 00:09:45.890
in real time.

00:09:45.890 --> 00:09:51.970
So the add-on displays
information in a little sidebar

00:09:51.970 --> 00:09:56.120
and also updates [? metrics ?]
that you scored on, letting you

00:09:56.120 --> 00:09:57.030
know--

00:09:57.030 --> 00:10:01.100
so how many people have
either opened, or clicked,

00:10:01.100 --> 00:10:04.090
on your email.

00:10:04.090 --> 00:10:07.632
OK, so, that's the--

00:10:07.632 --> 00:10:13.422
that was just an introduction
to explain how the--

00:10:13.422 --> 00:10:17.810
what Yet Another
Mail Merge does.

00:10:17.810 --> 00:10:22.740
As we have a lot of
users, we thought

00:10:22.740 --> 00:10:27.660
it was interesting to
track usage and learn how

00:10:27.660 --> 00:10:30.530
people are using our add-on.

00:10:30.530 --> 00:10:34.660
And for that, since
more than a year now,

00:10:34.660 --> 00:10:39.780
we are heavily using
Google Analytics because it

00:10:39.780 --> 00:10:46.440
lets us see the exact number
of users using the add-on

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:51.120
every month, day, and so
on, and to track the usage

00:10:51.120 --> 00:10:52.930
of different features.

00:10:52.930 --> 00:10:57.990
So before going to the
Stackdriver Logging part,

00:10:57.990 --> 00:11:02.740
I want to show what we were
using before Stackdriver,

00:11:02.740 --> 00:11:08.100
and when we decided
to switch and--

00:11:08.100 --> 00:11:14.840
what decided us to switch some
parts from Google Analytics

00:11:14.840 --> 00:11:18.270
to Stackdriver.

00:11:18.270 --> 00:11:22.110
So in Google Analytics
you are able to easily see

00:11:22.110 --> 00:11:26.550
you're active users, the
number of daily active users,

00:11:26.550 --> 00:11:31.200
seven day active users,
or monthly active users.

00:11:31.200 --> 00:11:37.550
You are able to compare over
time those users, so you

00:11:37.550 --> 00:11:40.740
are able to see if
the number of users

00:11:40.740 --> 00:11:46.200
is increasing from
month to month.

00:11:46.200 --> 00:11:49.102
So if you are on a gross--

00:11:49.102 --> 00:11:52.770
or if there are some issues
because less and less people

00:11:52.770 --> 00:11:55.730
are using your add-on.

00:11:55.730 --> 00:11:59.840
We are also able to
track several events.

00:11:59.840 --> 00:12:04.160
So, for example, as
Yet Another Mail Merge

00:12:04.160 --> 00:12:08.370
is a mail merge tool to
send email campaigns,

00:12:08.370 --> 00:12:12.260
we are sending to
analytics information

00:12:12.260 --> 00:12:14.180
about those e-mail campaigns.

00:12:14.180 --> 00:12:18.740
So we are able to see how
many email campaigns were sent

00:12:18.740 --> 00:12:23.960
over a specific period of time,
how many e-mails were sent

00:12:23.960 --> 00:12:28.000
per campaign, and as a total.

00:12:28.000 --> 00:12:34.600
And we can also send a lot more
information to Google Analytics

00:12:34.600 --> 00:12:39.350
to get information about which
features people are using,

00:12:39.350 --> 00:12:39.850
and so on.

00:12:39.850 --> 00:12:43.000
So, for example, I
presented earlier

00:12:43.000 --> 00:12:48.430
that, in your Gmail draft, you
can put placeholder makers so

00:12:48.430 --> 00:12:51.280
that the email
won't look exactly

00:12:51.280 --> 00:12:55.720
the same for every user, but
will be a bit personalized.

00:12:55.720 --> 00:12:59.710
But not everybody is using it.

00:12:59.710 --> 00:13:05.290
In fact, yes, more
than 36% of all users

00:13:05.290 --> 00:13:09.380
are sending the exact
same message to everybody.

00:13:09.380 --> 00:13:13.090
And I don't have
that on the slides,

00:13:13.090 --> 00:13:16.100
but that's something we
have actually improved.

00:13:16.100 --> 00:13:20.080
So there were a
lot more people not

00:13:20.080 --> 00:13:22.880
using any kind of
personalization before,

00:13:22.880 --> 00:13:27.850
and because we track
this kind of information,

00:13:27.850 --> 00:13:32.290
we were able to better
indicate to people how

00:13:32.290 --> 00:13:34.860
to personalize their e-mails.

00:13:34.860 --> 00:13:38.150
And once they knew
that that was possible,

00:13:38.150 --> 00:13:40.540
they used that feature--

00:13:40.540 --> 00:13:45.070
so the ratio has
increased in favor

00:13:45.070 --> 00:13:52.520
of more people are using
placeholders as time goes by.

00:13:52.520 --> 00:13:55.210
It's the same for our
email tracking feature.

00:13:55.210 --> 00:13:58.180
Now pretty much
everyone is using it,

00:13:58.180 --> 00:14:01.210
and before it was not the case.

00:14:01.210 --> 00:14:04.310
Thanks to all the data
we are collecting,

00:14:04.310 --> 00:14:10.000
we were able to see where we
needed to make improvements

00:14:10.000 --> 00:14:15.630
to be sure that people know
that the feature existed,

00:14:15.630 --> 00:14:18.970
and that it was useful for them.

00:14:18.970 --> 00:14:22.690
And we're tracking
several data like that,

00:14:22.690 --> 00:14:29.140
mostly thanks to the ability
to create custom dimension

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:35.440
in Google Analytics that lets
you send specific data related

00:14:35.440 --> 00:14:37.380
to your app-- your add-on--

00:14:37.380 --> 00:14:38.970
to analytics.

00:14:38.970 --> 00:14:46.300
So if people have activated
one feature in your app,

00:14:46.300 --> 00:14:48.840
also to categorize people.

00:14:48.840 --> 00:14:54.820
So, is it mostly used
by gmail.com users?

00:14:54.820 --> 00:14:55.870
By G Suite users?

00:14:59.350 --> 00:15:03.700
How many people are using, and
also paying, for the product?

00:15:03.700 --> 00:15:05.740
It's a premium
product so some people

00:15:05.740 --> 00:15:10.030
are using it and sending emails
for free, and some are paying.

00:15:10.030 --> 00:15:11.910
What is the
proportion, and so on.

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:16.240
So, all that were very
interesting metric--

00:15:16.240 --> 00:15:17.810
interesting metrics for us.

00:15:17.810 --> 00:15:24.460
And we improve our usage of
Google Analytics over time.

00:15:24.460 --> 00:15:30.040
And so we were also using Google
Analytics to track errors.

00:15:30.040 --> 00:15:37.600
So that was good because
we were able to see, thanks

00:15:37.600 --> 00:15:42.040
to all the chats and default
reportings available in Google

00:15:42.040 --> 00:15:46.770
Analytics, directly the number
of times a specific error

00:15:46.770 --> 00:15:49.910
happened, the number
of users impacted.

00:15:49.910 --> 00:15:54.220
And so we were able
to know on which error

00:15:54.220 --> 00:16:00.940
we needed to be proactive
and find a fix quickly.

00:16:00.940 --> 00:16:06.430
But, apart from that,
sending errors to analytics

00:16:06.430 --> 00:16:09.040
is a bit restrictive,
because you can only

00:16:09.040 --> 00:16:14.470
send a simple string
of errors, and you

00:16:14.470 --> 00:16:17.620
don't have all the
stack of the error,

00:16:17.620 --> 00:16:23.710
and it's not really great to
investigate and understand

00:16:23.710 --> 00:16:27.500
why this error is happening.

00:16:27.500 --> 00:16:36.250
So that's why it would be better
to activate cloud logging,

00:16:36.250 --> 00:16:39.470
and that's what we did.

00:16:39.470 --> 00:16:43.330
We activated in Yet Another
Mail Merge the connection

00:16:43.330 --> 00:16:45.260
with Stackdriver.

00:16:45.260 --> 00:16:51.130
And now we are able to see,
in real time, all the errors

00:16:51.130 --> 00:16:53.080
happening on the add-on.

00:16:53.080 --> 00:16:56.570
So every time someone
is using the add-on

00:16:56.570 --> 00:17:01.750
and is generating an error, we
can see the log in real time,

00:17:01.750 --> 00:17:03.900
and we can click
on a specific log

00:17:03.900 --> 00:17:07.089
to have all the
information about the log.

00:17:07.089 --> 00:17:13.609
So for which user is
impacted, on which function,

00:17:13.609 --> 00:17:17.710
at which specific line
of code in the script,

00:17:17.710 --> 00:17:19.450
and so on and so on.

00:17:19.450 --> 00:17:24.190
All those information are really
helpful to investigate and find

00:17:24.190 --> 00:17:26.890
a solution.

00:17:26.890 --> 00:17:32.800
So the question was, was
it interesting for us

00:17:32.800 --> 00:17:35.710
to switch to
Stackdriver Logging,

00:17:35.710 --> 00:17:39.580
knowing that we already
have a lot of stuff

00:17:39.580 --> 00:17:43.120
in place with Google Analytics?

00:17:43.120 --> 00:17:49.480
Was it-- in fact, does it
make sense to totally forget

00:17:49.480 --> 00:17:53.980
about Google Analytics
and go all Stackdriver,

00:17:53.980 --> 00:17:56.720
or should we use both of them?

00:17:56.720 --> 00:18:00.190
And we're trying to
answer that question.

00:18:00.190 --> 00:18:04.840
So all the advantages
of Stackdriver

00:18:04.840 --> 00:18:10.420
is that it's, well, it's easier
to see all errors leading

00:18:10.420 --> 00:18:12.340
to a specific user.

00:18:12.340 --> 00:18:16.960
In Google Analytics, you must
[? have ?] aggregated data,

00:18:16.960 --> 00:18:22.180
but it's difficult to
see precisely what is

00:18:22.180 --> 00:18:24.450
happening for a specific user.

00:18:24.450 --> 00:18:28.720
With Stackdriver we can
enter a specific user key,

00:18:28.720 --> 00:18:31.630
link to a user, and
we can see everything

00:18:31.630 --> 00:18:34.390
that's happening for
the specific user.

00:18:34.390 --> 00:18:40.090
Plus, we do have
all the stack traces

00:18:40.090 --> 00:18:45.080
to have all the details
about the error.

00:18:45.080 --> 00:18:46.910
And it's really easy to use.

00:18:46.910 --> 00:18:48.880
In fact, we don't
have-- we only have

00:18:48.880 --> 00:18:52.855
to check a box in the
app script editor,

00:18:52.855 --> 00:18:57.910
and all the errors start
flowing into Stackdriver.

00:18:57.910 --> 00:19:01.330
And also, the logs are
available in real time.

00:19:01.330 --> 00:19:07.390
So that has been very
helpful in the recent weeks

00:19:07.390 --> 00:19:10.780
when we deployed a new
version of the add-on

00:19:10.780 --> 00:19:19.280
because it's not always possible
to launch perfect updates.

00:19:19.280 --> 00:19:22.930
And if you see new
errors, you are

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:28.900
able to quickly react and
updates and push a new version.

00:19:28.900 --> 00:19:33.850
The limitation that we saw
with Stackdriver Logging

00:19:33.850 --> 00:19:43.810
is mostly that there's no
real metric capability.

00:19:43.810 --> 00:19:49.300
So this means that it's
not easy, or not possible,

00:19:49.300 --> 00:19:53.860
to directly, from
Stackdriver Logging UI,

00:19:53.860 --> 00:19:58.040
see the number of people
impacted by a specific error,

00:19:58.040 --> 00:20:05.260
or see the top triggering
errors on your script and so on.

00:20:05.260 --> 00:20:09.730
The good news is that it's
possible to export logs

00:20:09.730 --> 00:20:10.230
to BigQuery.

00:20:10.230 --> 00:20:17.530
And when you do that, it
solves the previous limitation

00:20:17.530 --> 00:20:22.960
because we can aggregate the
data available in Stackdriver

00:20:22.960 --> 00:20:26.660
Logging in BigQuery
in any way we want.

00:20:26.660 --> 00:20:30.520
And that's what I
will show you now.

00:20:30.520 --> 00:20:34.060
So it's very easy
to set up export

00:20:34.060 --> 00:20:39.340
to BigQuery in
the cloud console.

00:20:39.340 --> 00:20:46.120
You only have to click on a
few buttons to set that up.

00:20:46.120 --> 00:20:50.110
And once that is
set up, the logs

00:20:50.110 --> 00:20:56.111
are sent to BigQuery in real
time using streaming inserts.

00:20:56.111 --> 00:21:00.110
A new table is
created every day.

00:21:00.110 --> 00:21:05.250
You don't have to create the
table schema, or anything.

00:21:05.250 --> 00:21:07.900
And it's good because
you can decide

00:21:07.900 --> 00:21:13.650
if you want to keep
all the logs forever,

00:21:13.650 --> 00:21:20.470
or you can set up expiration
dates on the tables

00:21:20.470 --> 00:21:24.340
to be sure that you
won't be invoiced

00:21:24.340 --> 00:21:29.320
too much at the end of the
month because BigQuery has

00:21:29.320 --> 00:21:32.320
a free tier, but
if you are storing

00:21:32.320 --> 00:21:36.490
too much data in BigQuery,
it's going to cost you

00:21:36.490 --> 00:21:38.480
something at some point.

00:21:38.480 --> 00:21:42.220
So usually those
kinds of logs are

00:21:42.220 --> 00:21:47.400
useful for a few days or
months, or something like that.

00:21:47.400 --> 00:21:49.390
And it's possible
to say to BigQuery,

00:21:49.390 --> 00:21:52.320
"OK, I only want to keep
this data for a month,

00:21:52.320 --> 00:21:54.610
and that's all."

00:21:54.610 --> 00:21:58.350
Also, directly in
Stackdriver, if you

00:21:58.350 --> 00:22:00.910
are using the free
version of Stackdriver,

00:22:00.910 --> 00:22:04.160
logs are only kept
for seven days.

00:22:04.160 --> 00:22:12.190
So if you want to increase that,
using the BigQuery integration

00:22:12.190 --> 00:22:17.560
does help, because you can save
all logs forever if you want.

00:22:20.350 --> 00:22:24.280
So once the data
are in BigQuery,

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:28.780
you can make
inquiries, as I said.

00:22:28.780 --> 00:22:32.780
So the logs are split
in daily tables,

00:22:32.780 --> 00:22:35.260
but it's easy to
perform inquiries

00:22:35.260 --> 00:22:38.950
over multiple daily table
within a day trench.

00:22:38.950 --> 00:22:44.980
So it means that you don't have
to look at the errors per day,

00:22:44.980 --> 00:22:48.730
but you can look at the
errors over a period of days.

00:22:51.490 --> 00:22:55.960
So, for example, among
the useful queries,

00:22:55.960 --> 00:23:02.920
I will explain a bit right
after how we write our queries.

00:23:02.920 --> 00:23:08.420
But among the useful queries
we have you can, for example,

00:23:08.420 --> 00:23:11.100
see the number of
errors happening

00:23:11.100 --> 00:23:15.350
every day and the number
of impacted users.

00:23:15.350 --> 00:23:21.760
So that's just basic statistics,
but it doesn't tell you

00:23:21.760 --> 00:23:23.650
where it's happening exactly.

00:23:23.650 --> 00:23:32.710
So you can also list the most
recurring errors on the script,

00:23:32.710 --> 00:23:38.650
and also look at the
number of impacted users

00:23:38.650 --> 00:23:41.320
for those errors.

00:23:41.320 --> 00:23:44.200
So you will know which--

00:23:44.200 --> 00:23:50.290
which error to fix, while
some of-- most of those errors

00:23:50.290 --> 00:23:52.620
are errors happening
on Google Sites.

00:23:52.620 --> 00:24:03.610
And it's just a matter of adding
more retrial when it fails.

00:24:03.610 --> 00:24:06.370
It lets you quickly see
if there's something

00:24:06.370 --> 00:24:11.530
you can fix to improve the app.

00:24:11.530 --> 00:24:17.150
You can see, also, the number
of errors per function name.

00:24:17.150 --> 00:24:24.220
So to see, also, if a specific
function contains more issues

00:24:24.220 --> 00:24:28.400
than others, and to
which part of your code

00:24:28.400 --> 00:24:36.750
you should take care most,
based on all those data.

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:40.670
And we are also able to
see the number of errors

00:24:40.670 --> 00:24:43.430
generated by each user.

00:24:43.430 --> 00:24:46.750
As Paul explained
previously, when

00:24:46.750 --> 00:24:52.220
Apps Script is automatically
sending data to Stackdriver,

00:24:52.220 --> 00:24:55.670
each time it's sending
a new error log,

00:24:55.670 --> 00:24:59.320
it provides the user
key for specific users.

00:24:59.320 --> 00:25:04.340
So we're able to see if some
users are encountering way

00:25:04.340 --> 00:25:08.030
more errors than other people,
and then investigate on

00:25:08.030 --> 00:25:13.670
that because sometimes people
do have a lot of errors,

00:25:13.670 --> 00:25:17.150
but aren't reporting
an issue to us.

00:25:17.150 --> 00:25:20.850
So it's useful to understand
what is happening.

00:25:20.850 --> 00:25:22.790
And maybe that's
something we can

00:25:22.790 --> 00:25:31.770
fix because we are simply not
thinking about the specific use

00:25:31.770 --> 00:25:32.600
case.

00:25:32.600 --> 00:25:37.160
And so this will help
us investigate and see

00:25:37.160 --> 00:25:40.940
that people are using the
add-on in a specific way

00:25:40.940 --> 00:25:43.850
that we haven't
thought of, and we can

00:25:43.850 --> 00:25:47.360
apply a fix to make it work.

00:25:47.360 --> 00:25:51.840
OK, so, if we take a look
at the query syntax--

00:25:51.840 --> 00:25:56.870
so first, when you want to
make a query in BigQuery,

00:25:56.870 --> 00:26:02.030
you have to indicate the
source data to be queried.

00:26:02.030 --> 00:26:12.280
So usually it begins with the
ID of the project on the cloud

00:26:12.280 --> 00:26:13.580
console.

00:26:13.580 --> 00:26:17.960
Every Apps Script project
is linked to a cloud console

00:26:17.960 --> 00:26:24.890
project where you can
find the Stackdriver logs.

00:26:24.890 --> 00:26:29.840
So for-- in the case of
Yet Another Mail Merge,

00:26:29.840 --> 00:26:34.130
we have the YAMM.app_scripts
console logs that are

00:26:34.130 --> 00:26:38.180
automatically created
when we have activated

00:26:38.180 --> 00:26:42.610
the synchronization between
Stackdriver and BigQuery.

00:26:45.290 --> 00:26:48.230
Here we can indicate
with BigQuery

00:26:48.230 --> 00:26:52.250
that we want to query
multiple daily table

00:26:52.250 --> 00:26:54.200
over a specific date range.

00:26:54.200 --> 00:26:57.400
So after that, I
will simply tell

00:26:57.400 --> 00:27:06.470
that I want to query the tables
from now to the last seven days

00:27:06.470 --> 00:27:12.530
to have all the
most fresh errors.

00:27:12.530 --> 00:27:18.950
I can do my Select, and indicate
if I want to display issues

00:27:18.950 --> 00:27:22.160
by date, according to
the number of errors,

00:27:22.160 --> 00:27:27.920
and indicate also
the users impacted.

00:27:27.920 --> 00:27:32.960
So we can retrieve
the user key, as we

00:27:32.960 --> 00:27:36.440
were saying before,
that is always

00:27:36.440 --> 00:27:40.560
passed by Apps Scripts for
each log sent to the driver.

00:27:43.190 --> 00:27:47.940
Also, we can indicate that
we want only to investigate

00:27:47.940 --> 00:27:52.380
on errors, knowing
that Stackdriver

00:27:52.380 --> 00:27:57.180
lets you log errors, but
also other information.

00:27:57.180 --> 00:28:01.200
And we will see that very soon.

00:28:01.200 --> 00:28:03.770
We can also do a group by to--

00:28:03.770 --> 00:28:09.110
group by to get a date
and get some aggregates.

00:28:09.110 --> 00:28:17.070
OK, so, exports to BigQuery
are a very nice addition.

00:28:17.070 --> 00:28:21.180
It's not available
in Google Analytics.

00:28:21.180 --> 00:28:28.650
So Google Analytics only
provides sample data,

00:28:28.650 --> 00:28:31.290
and sometimes it's not enough.

00:28:31.290 --> 00:28:34.770
If you want an integration
between Google Analytics

00:28:34.770 --> 00:28:37.560
and BigQuery, you
have to go to--

00:28:37.560 --> 00:28:41.130
you have to pay for Google
Analytics Premium, which

00:28:41.130 --> 00:28:44.210
is very, very expensive.

00:28:44.210 --> 00:28:49.110
And so, the ability
to use Stackdriver,

00:28:49.110 --> 00:28:56.740
with it's free integration,
with BigQuery is quite useful.

00:28:56.740 --> 00:29:04.400
So we decided that, as Google
Analytics do provide charts

00:29:04.400 --> 00:29:08.670
and a lot of metrics
right out of the box,

00:29:08.670 --> 00:29:13.350
it makes sense to continue
to use Analytics to track

00:29:13.350 --> 00:29:19.830
our number of monthly
users, the usage of values

00:29:19.830 --> 00:29:24.750
feature in the add-on,
but it made total sense

00:29:24.750 --> 00:29:28.800
to stop plugging errors
in Google Analytics

00:29:28.800 --> 00:29:31.590
and start logging
them in Stackdriver.

00:29:34.635 --> 00:29:37.510
OK, and now we
will see how we can

00:29:37.510 --> 00:29:44.060
send more logs to Stackdriver,
in addition to errors,

00:29:44.060 --> 00:29:49.600
to help you when you want to
push updates, or understand how

00:29:49.600 --> 00:29:52.930
people are using the add-on.

00:29:52.930 --> 00:29:57.040
So first I will
do a little demo,

00:29:57.040 --> 00:30:00.340
just because we
haven't showed yet

00:30:00.340 --> 00:30:06.670
how easy it is to integrate
Apps Script and Stackdriver.

00:30:06.670 --> 00:30:14.230
So, as we said
before, for now it's

00:30:14.230 --> 00:30:18.980
only available as
part of EAP slash

00:30:18.980 --> 00:30:24.340
trusted desktop program,
so you don't have it yet.

00:30:24.340 --> 00:30:32.890
But it's rather easy to add
a new line to log information

00:30:32.890 --> 00:30:34.790
in Apps Script.

00:30:34.790 --> 00:30:42.910
Here, I will just send a little
information to cloud logging.

00:30:42.910 --> 00:30:49.720
So if I run this function,
it logs automatically

00:30:49.720 --> 00:30:52.770
this information in
Stackdriver Logging,

00:30:52.770 --> 00:30:56.330
and I can then click on
the view console logs.

00:30:56.330 --> 00:31:01.720
And, as it's the first time-- as
it's a new Apps Script project,

00:31:01.720 --> 00:31:05.900
I haven't actually
enabled logging before.

00:31:05.900 --> 00:31:10.300
So Apps Script is
asking me if I want

00:31:10.300 --> 00:31:14.710
to enable exception logging
for all future execution

00:31:14.710 --> 00:31:15.610
of the script.

00:31:15.610 --> 00:31:18.130
And that's very
useful because this

00:31:18.130 --> 00:31:23.860
means that without adding
any more lines of code,

00:31:23.860 --> 00:31:27.730
every time the script
produces an error,

00:31:27.730 --> 00:31:33.230
it will be automatically sent to
cloud-- to Stackdriver Logging.

00:31:33.230 --> 00:31:40.180
So here it lets me open
the console project linked

00:31:40.180 --> 00:31:42.430
to my Apps Script protect--

00:31:42.430 --> 00:31:45.370
opens directly in
this console project--

00:31:45.370 --> 00:31:49.150
my [? in this cloud ?]
project Stackdriver Logging,

00:31:49.150 --> 00:31:52.810
and I can see the
information I just sent.

00:31:52.810 --> 00:31:57.090
Where is the timestamp, my
user key for this project,

00:31:57.090 --> 00:32:03.650
so if multiple users
are using the script,

00:32:03.650 --> 00:32:07.840
they will of their own user key.

00:32:07.840 --> 00:32:13.230
I can see that the--

00:32:13.230 --> 00:32:18.130
it has-- the log has been
sent by the function name

00:32:18.130 --> 00:32:21.060
my function, that the
[? invocation ?] type is

00:32:21.060 --> 00:32:23.710
editor, and you--

00:32:23.710 --> 00:32:30.470
it's also makes a difference
if logs are coming from--

00:32:30.470 --> 00:32:32.380
time drive and trigger--

00:32:32.380 --> 00:32:37.330
from a UI of your add-on in the
spreadsheet-- in the sidebar--

00:32:37.330 --> 00:32:38.380
and so on.

00:32:38.380 --> 00:32:41.710
So it's quite useful
to exactly see

00:32:41.710 --> 00:32:45.640
how the error happened exactly.

00:32:45.640 --> 00:32:51.250
And so here it's not an
error, it's a simple log.

00:32:51.250 --> 00:32:55.220
If I want to generate an
error, it's very simple.

00:32:55.220 --> 00:32:59.140
So here, for example,
a mistake in the code.

00:32:59.140 --> 00:33:02.370
I don't-- I haven't put that
console.log but console.org.

00:33:02.370 --> 00:33:06.280
And so If I start
streaming the log,

00:33:06.280 --> 00:33:09.160
I should be able
to find the errors.

00:33:09.160 --> 00:33:15.190
You can see that here I haven't
entered any new line of code,

00:33:15.190 --> 00:33:18.940
not to try [? catch, ?]
or anything, to send

00:33:18.940 --> 00:33:21.610
the error back to Stackdriver.

00:33:21.610 --> 00:33:25.140
It's just that among
the project properties,

00:33:25.140 --> 00:33:29.650
log exceptions is checked
now, automatically.

00:33:29.650 --> 00:33:34.650
It means that every time the
script will show an error,

00:33:34.650 --> 00:33:39.370
I will be able to find
the error in Stackdriver.

00:33:39.370 --> 00:33:41.680
That's very useful
because you don't

00:33:41.680 --> 00:33:45.670
have to add any
complexity to your code,

00:33:45.670 --> 00:33:49.300
and you can benefit from
the feature right away.

00:33:49.300 --> 00:33:51.430
That's all for the demo.

00:33:51.430 --> 00:33:53.780
If I can go back to
the presentation,

00:33:53.780 --> 00:33:56.790
thank you very much.

00:33:56.790 --> 00:34:00.390
So in addition to
basic information

00:34:00.390 --> 00:34:02.820
like the [? error ?]
I just entered,

00:34:02.820 --> 00:34:06.780
it's also possible to
send additional info

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:09.130
as JSON objects.

00:34:09.130 --> 00:34:14.310
So, for example, here we
are collecting information

00:34:14.310 --> 00:34:18.690
about the number of e-mails
sent to better understand

00:34:18.690 --> 00:34:24.690
if people are able to send
all their emails in one batch,

00:34:24.690 --> 00:34:30.070
or if they have to do it in
multiple batches, and so on.

00:34:30.070 --> 00:34:33.610
So you can put a lot of
different information--

00:34:33.610 --> 00:34:39.190
and you will retrieve all this
information in Stackdriver

00:34:39.190 --> 00:34:43.139
Logging as JSON output.

00:34:43.139 --> 00:34:50.699
And you can then reuse all that
information to filter the logs.

00:34:50.699 --> 00:34:54.620
So if you see at the
top of the screen shots,

00:34:54.620 --> 00:35:00.300
there's a little
query bar where you

00:35:00.300 --> 00:35:04.470
can indicate that you want
to filter the data based

00:35:04.470 --> 00:35:08.640
on the JSON payload and the
data in the JSON payload.

00:35:11.690 --> 00:35:16.360
So that's really
useful, especially

00:35:16.360 --> 00:35:20.020
when you are unloading
the support for the app

00:35:20.020 --> 00:35:25.900
because if you need to see
everything that happened

00:35:25.900 --> 00:35:29.950
to a specific user who
is asking for help, well,

00:35:29.950 --> 00:35:34.120
we can easily add
information using--

00:35:34.120 --> 00:35:39.670
or consult that log at
every important point

00:35:39.670 --> 00:35:42.240
during the use of the add-on.

00:35:42.240 --> 00:35:45.520
And so, for example,
I can automatically

00:35:45.520 --> 00:35:48.610
collect the remaining
daily quota,

00:35:48.610 --> 00:35:53.410
and doing that I don't
send the email myself.

00:35:53.410 --> 00:35:57.340
I'm calling the Gmail
API to send emails

00:35:57.340 --> 00:36:02.470
that each user has a specific
quota to send emails every day.

00:36:02.470 --> 00:36:04.210
So I can check the
quota beforehand,

00:36:04.210 --> 00:36:09.670
and see how many emails he
will-- he was able to send,

00:36:09.670 --> 00:36:17.800
and the time needed to send
all those emails, the remaining

00:36:17.800 --> 00:36:20.840
quota after that,
and so on and so on.

00:36:20.840 --> 00:36:24.190
And with all those
information, when

00:36:24.190 --> 00:36:28.000
someone asks for help
on our support platform,

00:36:28.000 --> 00:36:33.720
we're able to check back
the logs for these users,

00:36:33.720 --> 00:36:38.320
and this is really
helpful to understand

00:36:38.320 --> 00:36:43.630
what he has done because
every user is not

00:36:43.630 --> 00:36:47.770
able to exactly describe what
he has done before having

00:36:47.770 --> 00:36:52.570
a specific issue, and we are
able to see all the history

00:36:52.570 --> 00:36:56.950
and better help him
understand what went wrong.

00:36:59.830 --> 00:37:07.230
Something-- we started using
Stackdriver Logging in part

00:37:07.230 --> 00:37:08.220
because--

00:37:08.220 --> 00:37:13.800
due to a change
in the Gmail EPI--

00:37:13.800 --> 00:37:19.950
a lot of users reported
that it took time

00:37:19.950 --> 00:37:22.390
to send email campaigns.

00:37:22.390 --> 00:37:26.070
And so we wanted to know
exactly how many people

00:37:26.070 --> 00:37:31.500
were impacted by slow sending
time for our email campaign.

00:37:31.500 --> 00:37:34.790
So we started to
tracking that exactly--

00:37:34.790 --> 00:37:36.060
to track that exactly.

00:37:36.060 --> 00:37:40.590
We added different
information to understand

00:37:40.590 --> 00:37:42.660
what could cause this issue.

00:37:42.660 --> 00:37:46.600
Could it be equally
different of the draft size?

00:37:46.600 --> 00:37:53.190
So, for example, if you change
a lot of images and attachments

00:37:53.190 --> 00:38:00.240
to your draft, so you end
up with a 20 megabyte draft

00:38:00.240 --> 00:38:05.220
that you will send
to 1,000 people,

00:38:05.220 --> 00:38:11.160
it means that you will, in
fact, send 20 gigabytes of data

00:38:11.160 --> 00:38:12.720
with your campaign.

00:38:12.720 --> 00:38:19.920
And so that might explain why
some people find it a bit slow.

00:38:19.920 --> 00:38:22.140
So in fact, it was
not the reason,

00:38:22.140 --> 00:38:26.370
and we were able to see that
it really simply depends

00:38:26.370 --> 00:38:30.730
on the number of emails
you want to send.

00:38:30.730 --> 00:38:33.040
Gmail-- the Gmail API--

00:38:33.040 --> 00:38:37.200
has specific
threshold to make sure

00:38:37.200 --> 00:38:41.160
that you are not sending
too many emails in a very

00:38:41.160 --> 00:38:42.450
short time.

00:38:42.450 --> 00:38:47.820
And based on all the data we
aggregated with Stackdriver,

00:38:47.820 --> 00:38:50.640
we're able to update
all documentation

00:38:50.640 --> 00:38:55.680
to indicate to people the time
needed to spend a campaign--

00:38:55.680 --> 00:38:59.850
to send a campaign based
on the recipients you add.

00:38:59.850 --> 00:39:04.080
And we were-- we were
able also to update

00:39:04.080 --> 00:39:09.780
add-on itself to warn users
before they send a campaign.

00:39:09.780 --> 00:39:14.690
So, for example, if they're
done with spreadsheets and want

00:39:14.690 --> 00:39:21.330
to start a campaign sending
e-mails to more than 100 and--

00:39:21.330 --> 00:39:27.630
1,200 users, we know that
it will take thirty minutes.

00:39:27.630 --> 00:39:31.170
So we display a
warning, it will take

00:39:31.170 --> 00:39:35.820
some time we have to leave your
computers open for some time,

00:39:35.820 --> 00:39:38.760
so do you want to
do it now or do

00:39:38.760 --> 00:39:41.370
you want to reschedule
your campaign, and so on.

00:39:45.561 --> 00:39:46.060
OK.

00:39:49.160 --> 00:39:52.650
We've displayed previously the--

00:39:52.650 --> 00:39:57.230
always the user
keys, so Apps Script

00:39:57.230 --> 00:40:01.840
generates a specific
user key for each user

00:40:01.840 --> 00:40:05.970
and then sends it to
Stackdriver Logging.

00:40:05.970 --> 00:40:12.920
But in fact, if a user sends
you a [? ship ?] request,

00:40:12.920 --> 00:40:18.220
you have to match this
user and his email address

00:40:18.220 --> 00:40:21.490
with a specific user key.

00:40:21.490 --> 00:40:24.720
And so in Yet
Another Mail Merge we

00:40:24.720 --> 00:40:28.820
are using Firebase as
our database, where

00:40:28.820 --> 00:40:33.710
we are storing user profiles.

00:40:33.710 --> 00:40:39.410
In each user profile we
are storing the user key,

00:40:39.410 --> 00:40:43.520
so that when someone
asks for help

00:40:43.520 --> 00:40:48.770
we are able to open his user
profile, get that user key,

00:40:48.770 --> 00:40:53.670
and use that to find all logs
related to the specific user.

00:40:53.670 --> 00:40:56.510
So to do that there's simply--

00:40:56.510 --> 00:41:00.760
in Apps Script, you can
call [? session ?] that

00:41:00.760 --> 00:41:05.060
get temporary active user
key, and send that information

00:41:05.060 --> 00:41:08.580
to your own database.

00:41:08.580 --> 00:41:12.990
So in our case, we are
sending that to Firebase.

00:41:12.990 --> 00:41:16.960
And so that's especially
useful to investigate when

00:41:16.960 --> 00:41:21.610
people are asking for help.

00:41:21.610 --> 00:41:26.770
Also, as it's yet
another tool added

00:41:26.770 --> 00:41:34.900
to add metrics and do
some analytics on usage

00:41:34.900 --> 00:41:40.240
of the add-on, you might
want to aggregate everything

00:41:40.240 --> 00:41:42.140
in a single dashboard.

00:41:42.140 --> 00:41:47.550
And while the good news is that
our studio is here to help,

00:41:47.550 --> 00:41:53.800
there have been some other
session about that studio,

00:41:53.800 --> 00:41:58.420
so I won't speak more
about it-- just that it

00:41:58.420 --> 00:42:02.050
is possible to build a single
dashboard where you will

00:42:02.050 --> 00:42:05.920
aggregate information
from Google Analytics,

00:42:05.920 --> 00:42:08.770
and information
from Stackdriver,

00:42:08.770 --> 00:42:13.760
so you have everything
in one single place.

00:42:13.760 --> 00:42:15.770
And it's not pretty,
but you can actually

00:42:15.770 --> 00:42:18.530
do a very pretty dashboard
with that studio.

00:42:21.050 --> 00:42:24.030
OK, so, some resources.

00:42:24.030 --> 00:42:29.000
So if you want to have access
to Stackdriver Logging,

00:42:29.000 --> 00:42:32.240
for now you have
to join the EAP.

00:42:32.240 --> 00:42:35.280
So you have
[INAUDIBLE] for that.

00:42:35.280 --> 00:42:37.970
There's also a
documentation explaining

00:42:37.970 --> 00:42:43.070
how to activate Stackdriver
Logging on your script

00:42:43.070 --> 00:42:46.400
and how to use it, especially
the console.log part,

00:42:46.400 --> 00:42:52.190
and everything outside of
the basic error logging.

00:42:52.190 --> 00:42:56.610
There are documentation
also on how to export

00:42:56.610 --> 00:42:59.390
Stackdriver logs to BigQuery--

00:42:59.390 --> 00:43:04.440
so how to set the
synchronization up,

00:43:04.440 --> 00:43:08.120
and also the query
reference, to know

00:43:08.120 --> 00:43:13.520
better how to query multiple
table over a [INAUDIBLE]

00:43:13.520 --> 00:43:17.360
and other things like that.

00:43:17.360 --> 00:43:18.990
At the beginning of
the presentation,

00:43:18.990 --> 00:43:23.170
I also spoke about
Google Analytics.

00:43:23.170 --> 00:43:26.350
If you also want to start
using Google Analytics

00:43:26.350 --> 00:43:30.920
in your add-ons, know that
there are some specificities,

00:43:30.920 --> 00:43:35.450
and so there is a blog post
available on the Google

00:43:35.450 --> 00:43:41.780
Developer blog about that,
about how to best use the user

00:43:41.780 --> 00:43:46.100
IDs and a lot of other
features in Google Analytics

00:43:46.100 --> 00:43:51.354
for indicates of
Apps Script add-ons.

00:43:51.354 --> 00:43:55.610
And if you're interested
in using Firebase

00:43:55.610 --> 00:44:00.440
as your database for your add-on
or any Apps Script project,

00:44:00.440 --> 00:44:04.610
there's a code library and
some examples that are also

00:44:04.610 --> 00:44:07.790
available at this address.

00:44:07.790 --> 00:44:08.960
PAUL MCREYNOLDS: Awesome.

00:44:08.960 --> 00:44:11.120
Thank you, Romain,
that was great.

00:44:11.120 --> 00:44:13.010
Thanks everybody for
coming late in the day.

00:44:13.010 --> 00:44:14.990
It was great to see you.

